1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,743
The fastest technical framing is this.

2
00:00:02,743 --> 00:00:10,757
Consentext is a control boundary that sits between stored health context and every outbound AI or share action.

3
00:00:10,757 --> 00:00:18,410
The vault holds context. Consent and policy decide what may move. The gateway enforces the selected route.

4
00:00:18,410 --> 00:00:25,702
Audit records what happened. The system is valuable because disclosure behavior stops being implicit.

5
00:00:25,702 --> 00:00:37,398
Instead of letting every request act like a raw export, the product classifies the request, chooses the correct lane, applies transforms, and leaves proof behind.

6
00:00:37,398 --> 00:00:44,040
That control boundary is the main idea the rest of the prototype is trying to make concrete.

7
00:00:44,040 --> 00:00:47,751
The problem is not just that health data is sensitive.

8
00:00:47,751 --> 00:00:53,455
The deeper problem is that most current AI flows make disclosure behavior implicit.

9
00:00:53,455 --> 00:01:05,207
A user pastes something into a tool, a system forwards context to a provider, or a share occurs with weak scoping, and nobody has a stable contract for what actually left.

10
00:01:05,207 --> 00:01:07,888
Consentext is trying to solve that gap.

11
00:01:07,888 --> 00:01:16,341
The user should be able to get useful explanation and stronger reasoning without every request turning into a broad export.

12
00:01:16,341 --> 00:01:26,856
The architecture matters because it creates explicit scope, explicit route selection, and explicit proof rather than relying on hand-wavy trust language.

13
00:01:26,856 --> 00:01:33,239
The most important architecture choice is the split between the vault and the control plane.

14
00:01:33,239 --> 00:01:39,066
The vault is where stored health context, raw artifacts, and derived summaries live.

15
00:01:39,066 --> 00:01:47,530
The control plane is where consent logic, policy objects, lane selection, minimization, de-identification, and audit live.

16
00:01:47,530 --> 00:01:52,248
The gateway then becomes the only governed path to external systems.

17
00:01:52,248 --> 00:01:58,838
That separation matters because storing context is not the same job as deciding what may leave.

18
00:01:58,838 --> 00:02:10,008
Once those responsibilities are separated, the system can fail closed, keep the policy object legible, and make outbound behavior inspectable instead of magical.

19
00:02:10,008 --> 00:02:17,484
Once the boundary is clear, the next idea is the lane model. Deterministic requests stay retrieval-only.

20
00:02:17,484 --> 00:02:26,109
Private requests use the internal protected model. Private Plus uses minimum-necessary or de-identified governed export.

21
00:02:26,109 --> 00:02:35,238
Max Intelligence uses broader user-authorized governed export. Off-Board models the moment the user leaves Consentext entirely.

22
00:02:35,238 --> 00:02:40,773
In implementation terms, the lane is the compact output of policy evaluation.

23
00:02:40,773 --> 00:02:53,352
It tells the rest of the system what class of context is allowed to move, which transforms apply, which gateway path is legal, and what kind of audit event should be appended.

24
00:02:53,352 --> 00:03:00,096
One of the most important March decisions is that Private and Private Plus are not interchangeable.

25
00:03:00,096 --> 00:03:02,549
Private is the locked internal lane.

26
00:03:02,549 --> 00:03:11,269
It requires a protected model running inside Consentext-controlled systems, and that is a phase requirement, not a nice-to-have.

27
00:03:11,269 --> 00:03:20,125
Private Plus is different. It is the narrow governed external lane where only minimum-necessary or de-identified context may move.

28
00:03:20,125 --> 00:03:27,073
The reason this distinction matters is that it changes real system behavior. Different transforms run.

29
00:03:27,073 --> 00:03:32,251
Different output classifications appear. Different provider paths are legal.

30
00:03:32,251 --> 00:03:37,224
If those two lanes collapse, the rest of the control plane loses meaning.

31
00:03:37,224 --> 00:03:42,049
Max Intelligence exists for the moments when a narrow route is not enough.

32
00:03:42,049 --> 00:03:50,004
A user may want deeper comparison, broader reasoning, or a more capable external model path than Private Plus can support.

33
00:03:50,004 --> 00:03:55,024
The important thing is that Consentext still stays in front of that exchange.

34
00:03:55,024 --> 00:04:03,044
The user authorizes broader context, the gateway still mediates provider access, and the audit log still records the route.

35
00:04:03,044 --> 00:04:10,869
That is what makes Max Intelligence different from Off-Board. Off-Board means the user leaves the protected environment.

36
00:04:10,869 --> 00:04:17,976
Max Intelligence means the system offers a broader governed lane without abandoning the control relationship.

37
00:04:17,976 --> 00:04:22,775
The prototype also has to show that this is more than an AI routing demo.

38
00:04:22,775 --> 00:04:27,640
A user-authorized clinician share path is now part of the system contract.

39
00:04:27,640 --> 00:04:36,647
That means a person can scope what should be sent to a doctor, clinic, or hospital without pretending a full staff portal already exists.

40
00:04:36,647 --> 00:04:42,301
The March nineteenth medication note adds an important launch boundary on top of that.

41
00:04:42,301 --> 00:04:55,055
Medication may exist in the record set and in the clinician packet, but launch AI does not offer dosing advice, medication recommendations, interaction clearance, or treatment-direction outputs.

42
00:04:55,055 --> 00:05:01,103
On top of that, the internal team needs enough operational visibility to support the system.

43
00:05:01,103 --> 00:05:09,518
They need event lookup, route search, recent risk review, and proof that a share or route behaved the way policy said it should.

44
00:05:09,518 --> 00:05:16,224
That is why the prototype now includes both the clinician packet preview and the internal ops console.

45
00:05:16,224 --> 00:05:23,728
A useful change in this pass is that the site no longer depends on a live presenter remembering every talking point.

46
00:05:23,728 --> 00:05:28,062
You can turn on Summary Mode to hide the denser prototype surfaces.

47
00:05:28,062 --> 00:05:32,396
You can switch to a lighter documentation theme for daytime review.

48
00:05:32,396 --> 00:05:42,358
You can open a one-page technical brief, download the narrated MP4 walkthrough, and export the current policy, audit summary, or clinician packet preview.

49
00:05:42,358 --> 00:05:53,808
That matters because it turns the prototype into a review package that a senior developer can inspect asynchronously, not just a live demo that disappears when the meeting ends.

50
00:05:53,808 --> 00:06:02,215
The implementation takeaway is this. Consentext is a policy engine plus governed gateway in front of health-context use.

51
00:06:02,215 --> 00:06:10,902
It separates data custody from disclosure logic, enforces distinct lane contracts, and records what happened as audit proof.

52
00:06:10,902 --> 00:06:25,334
The system gives users a safer internal path, a narrow governed external path, a broader governed path when they explicitly want it, and an honest model of what happens when they leave protections entirely.

53
00:06:25,334 --> 00:06:32,340
It also stays modular enough for later provider adapters, clinician workflows, and integration work.

54
00:06:32,340 --> 00:06:37,104
That is the architecture this walkthrough is trying to make legible.

